{"Title":"An Act relative to the examination of evidence rooms and evidentiary procedures","BillNumber":"H4596","DocketNumber":null,"GeneralCourtNumber":193,"PrimarySponsor":{"Id":"J19","Name":"Joint Committee on the Judiciary","Type":2,"Details":"https://malegislature.gov/api/GeneralCourts/193/Committees/J19","ResponseDate":"2024-04-30T11:55:45.397"},"Cosponsors":[{"Id":"MJC1","Name":"Mark J. Cusack","Type":1,"Details":"https://malegislature.gov/api/GeneralCourts/193/LegislativeMembers/MJC1","ResponseDate":"2023-01-18T13:49:48.9066667"}],"JointSponsor":null,"BillHistory":"https://malegislature.gov/api/GeneralCourts/193/Documents/H4596/DocumentHistoryActions","LegislationTypeName":"Bill","Pinslip":null,"DocumentText":"\tSECTION 1. Notwithstanding any general or special law to the contrary, there shall be established, pursuant to section 2A of chapter 4 of the General Laws, a special legislative commission known as the Commission on the Preservation and Storage of Evidence. The commission shall be comprised of the following 15 members: 6 members appointed by the governor, 1 of whom shall be an elected clerk of the superior court; 1 of whom shall be a clerk-magistrate of the district court; 1 of whom shall be a police officer with experience in evidence collection and preservation; 1 of whom shall be a criminal defense attorney;1 of whom shall be professor of evidence at a law school accredited by the American Bar Association and 1 of whom shall be a retired district court or boston municipal court justice; 3 members of the house of representatives, 1 of whom shall be appointed by the speaker of the house, 1 of whom shall be the house chair of the joint committee on the judiciary or designee and 1 of whom shall be appointed by the minority leader; 3 members of the senate, 1 of whom shall be appointed by the senate president, 1 of whom shall be the senate chair of the joint committee on the judiciary or designee and 1 of whom shall be appointed by the minority leader; the attorney general or a designee and 2 elected district attorneys appointed by the president of the Massachusetts District Attorneys Association. The house and senate chairs of the joint committee on the judiciary or their designees shall serve as co-chairs of the commission. \r\n\tThe commission shall conduct an examination of: (i) the existing legal and regulatory framework governing the preservation and storage of evidence and property collected by law enforcement; (ii) the current processes utilized by law enforcement for the preservation and storage of evidence and property including its submission, receipt, storage, disposition and use as part of court proceedings; and (iii) the current processes utilized by law enforcement for the storage of hazardous material; the protection and preservation of biological or DNA-related material; the adoption of security and chain of custody protocols; and the training of evidence or property personnel. \r\n\tThe commission shall investigate the feasibility and anticipated cost of constructing and maintaining a statewide evidence storage facility for the preservation and storage of evidence and property. \r\n\tIn undertaking its examinations and investigations the commission shall confer with representatives of the various state offices responsible for overseeing evidence collection and storage, as well as with academics, practitioners and others with expertise in these areas.\r\n\tThe commission shall file a report on all aspects of its examination and investigation with the governor, the president of the senate and the speaker of the house of representatives and the clerks of the house of representatives and senate regarding the results of its investigation and study on or before March 31, 2025. \r\n\tThe report shall include, but not be limited to: (i) an assessment of the effectiveness and sufficiency of current law and processes for evidence and property collection and storage including those used for hazardous material storage, the protection and preservation of biological/DNA-related material, security and chain of custody, and the training of evidence/property personnel; (ii) recommendations for improvement of these processes for use in their development of standardized guidelines for evidence and property management by law enforcement; (ii) recommendations on the feasibility and anticipated cost of constructing and maintaining a statewide evidence storage facility and evidence management program; and (iv) recommendations for improvements or amendments to any law, regulation or rule involving evidence or property collection and preservation including recommended legislation, if any.\r\n","EmergencyPreamble":null,"RollCalls":[],"Attachments":[],"CommitteeRecommendations":[{"Action":"Redraft","FiscalAmounts":[],"Committee":{"CommitteeCode":"J19","GeneralCourtNumber":193,"Details":"https://malegislature.gov/api/GeneralCourts/193/Committees/J19"},"Votes":[]},{"Action":"Discharge to Another Committee","FiscalAmounts":[],"Committee":{"CommitteeCode":"J40","GeneralCourtNumber":193,"Details":"https://malegislature.gov/api/GeneralCourts/193/Committees/J40"},"Votes":[]}],"Amendments":[]}